(1) Theorem 3.6 (penalised one-sided minimum distance), exact failure probability over all samples;
    'failure' = some eps-minimiser of c_N has TV(Q_est, Q) > min_i [3 TV(Q_i,Q) + 2 r_N(i)] + 2s + eps
    2600 random instances (2 to 4 hypotheses on 2 to 5 points, N <= 30, delta in {0.9, 0.5, 0.2, 0.05}, three choices of kappa,
    eps in {0, 0.02, 0.2}, lattice probabilities and duplicated measures included): instances with a positive failure probability: 77;
    largest failure probability / delta = 0.0205   (the theorem asserts <= 1)
    attained at (J, W, N, delta, kappa, eps, w, H, Q) = (3, 4, 8, 0.9, 0.799, 0.0, [0.3333, 0.3333, 0.3333], [[0.0, 0.1351, 0.7721, 0.0929], [0.1672, 0.5747, 0.0202, 0.2379], [0.0, 0.1351, 0.7721, 0.0929]], [0.0, 0.1351, 0.7721, 0.0929])
(1b) hill climbing over two hypotheses on {0,1} (N <= 45, delta down to 0.01, kappa = ln(2/delta) and 0): largest failure probability / delta = 0.1257
     at (N, delta, kappa, w1, w2, p1, p2, q) = (30, 0.9, 0.799, 0.9, 0.09999999999999998, 0.0317, 0.4949, 0.4936)
(2) Theorem 3.3 (the draft's estimator), human in the class, no further tests, 815 random instances, exact:
    largest Pr[the human's index does not match, or the highest-weight match has TV > sqrt((4K ln2 + 2 ln(2/delta))/N)] / delta = 0.0490  at (J, W, N, delta, Pr[human does not match]) = (2, 3, 30, 0.5, 0.02449)
(3) Proposition 3.8 (highest-weight admissible index), human in the class, 813 random instances, exact:
    largest Pr[the human's index is not admissible, or the estimate has TV > 2 r_N(human)] / delta = 0.1389  at (J, W, N, delta) = (3, 4, 2, 0.9)
