(5) Example 3.11(b) (replay of the data), u answers, human uniform, the hypotheses 'uniform on a set S of N answers':
    u = 60, N = 3: Pr[all answers distinct] = 0.9506; without penalty the replay has criterion 0 and TV 0.9500 from the human; with penalty r(Q_S) = 1.535 against 1 + r(Q) = 1.784: replay excluded: False; the file's condition u > N e^(2(1+r(Q))^2) = 1745: False
    u = 2000, N = 3: Pr[all answers distinct] = 0.9985; without penalty the replay has criterion 0 and TV 0.9985 from the human; with penalty r(Q_S) = 2.029 against 1 + r(Q) = 1.784: replay excluded: True; the file's condition u > N e^(2(1+r(Q))^2) = 1745: True
    u = 1000000, N = 5: Pr[all answers distinct] = 1.0000; without penalty the replay has criterion 0 and TV 1.0000 from the human; with penalty r(Q_S) = 2.607 against 1 + r(Q) = 1.607: replay excluded: True; the file's condition u > N e^(2(1+r(Q))^2) = 877: True
(6) Example 3.11(c): two hypotheses with nu(1) = 1/4, 3/4, human Q(1) = 1/2, exact:
    N =   10: Pr[no index matches (Theorem 3.3)] = 0.0000;  Pr[no index is admissible (Proposition 3.8)] = 0.0000
    N =   40: Pr[no index matches (Theorem 3.3)] = 0.1254;  Pr[no index is admissible (Proposition 3.8)] = 0.3642
    N =  160: Pr[no index matches (Theorem 3.3)] = 0.9994;  Pr[no index is admissible (Proposition 3.8)] = 0.9997
    N =  640: Pr[no index matches (Theorem 3.3)] = 1.0000;  Pr[no index is admissible (Proposition 3.8)] = 1.0000
(7) A human who is consistent within a run (scaffold: the same question twice; the human answers a fair bit and repeats it):
    run law of the human: (0,0) and (1,1) with probability 1/2 each; best stateless sampler: p = 0.29289, TV = 0.41422 (= sqrt(2) - 1 = 0.41421);
    the approximation term of Theorem 3.6 is 3 x 0.4142 = 1.2426 > 1: the oracle inequality is empty for every class of stateless samplers.
(8) Theorem 3.10 with all simulations driven by one shared set of random numbers (N = N_s = 60, 5 hypotheses, 3000 trials): failures 0 (allowed 20%)
